#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119616 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98bd56e8-77ca-553a-9c26-04feae0618dd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "adabb3ab-fc9c-5857-95ac-008bc560b257" :question/math-academy-id "e-8203" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(2,2^2)$, compute $P(X < 1.24)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, -0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3192 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3156 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3121 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3557 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3520 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3483 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3936 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3897 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3859 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$, we have\n\n$$\nP(X < x) = P \\left(Z < \\dfrac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu = 2$ and $\\sigma = 2$.\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 1.24) &= P(Z < \\frac{1.24 - 2}{2}) \\\\\n&= P(Z < - 0.38) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le - 0.38) \\\\\n&= Φ(-0.38)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1843/Source/Images/c5a42be64d06c7297fa9cb3c052995a7.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(-0.38) = 0.3520\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X < 1.24) = 0.3520\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119617 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b6aec2d-db43-5ff5-bd1f-1d5b38bde7e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/id #uuid "dad97320-cddd-501a-81bd-371090cc5be5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8204" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Y \\sim N(5,4^2)$, compute $P(Y \\geq 2.92)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, -0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2420 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2743 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2709 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2676 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3085 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3050 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3015 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $Y \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y \\geq y) = P \\left(Z \\geq \\dfrac{y-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu = 5$ and $\\sigma = 4$.\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Y \\ge 2.92) &= P(Z \\ge \\frac{2.92 - 5}{4}) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\ge - 0.52)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1843/Source/Images/e696c5f6c514a84f59483d545326ddec.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Z \\ge - 0.52) &= 1 - P(Z < - 0.52) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z \\le - 0.52) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(-0.52)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(-0.52) =0.3015\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Z \\ge - 0.52) &= 1 - Φ(-0.52) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.3015 \\\\\n&= 0.6985\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y \\geq 2.92) = 0.6985\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119618 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af01940d-f4ef-53a2-b7e6-3ac45a9beb0c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "ab98835e-cf10-5fd3-b42b-fa50d8d95967" :question/math-academy-id "e-8205" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(1,9)$, compute $P(-1.13 < X < 3.25)$.\n\n| $z$$\\, 0.00 \\,$$\\, 0.01 \\,$$\\, 0.02 \\,$$\\, -0.9 \\,$ $\\, 0.1841 \\,$ $\\, 0.1814 \\,$ $\\, 0.1788 \\,$ $\\, -0.8 \\,$ $\\, 0.2119 \\,$ $\\, 0.2090 \\,$ $\\, 0.2061 \\,$ $\\, -0.7 \\,$ $\\, 0.2420 \\,$ $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ |  | $z$$\\, 0.03 \\,$$\\, 0.04 \\,$$\\, 0.05 \\,$$\\, 0.5 \\,$ $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ $\\, 0.7088 \\,$ $\\, 0.6 \\,$ $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ $\\, 0.7 \\,$ $\\, 0.7673 \\,$ $\\, 0.7704 \\,$ $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$, we have\n\n$$\nP(a < X < b) = P \\left(\\dfrac{a-\\mu}{\\sigma} < Z < \\dfrac{b-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu = 1$ and\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt 9 = 3\n$$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-1.13 < X < 3.25) &= P(\\frac{-1.13 - 1}{3} < Z < \\frac{3.25 - 1}{3}) \\\\\n&= P(-0.71 < Z < 0.75)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1843/Source/Images/9082689cdb56dc5b0cdb463ce1690c15.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-0.71 < Z < 0.75) &= P(Z < 0.75) - P(Z \\le - 0.71) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 0.75) - P(Z \\le - 0.71) \\\\\n&= Φ(0.75) - Φ(-0.71)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ(-0.71) &= 0.2389 \\\\\nΦ(0.75) &= 0.7734\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-0.71 < Z < 0.75) &= Φ(0.75) - Φ(-0.71) \\\\\n&= 0.7734 - 0.2389 \\\\\n&= 0.5345\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(-1.13< X < 3.25) = 0.5345\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af01940d-f4ef-53a2-b7e6-3ac45a9beb0c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec263f97-5b7f-55f5-9565-f7fed6c4262f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0214" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119355} {:answer/id #uuid "151009f4-ca1f-57d2-bb04-54e10c847b8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0546" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119356} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee4df29-08bd-5f90-9bb8-b838e5cad9c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0342" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119357} {:answer/id #uuid "6d48d27e-de4d-5e3f-a723-dd51ce5407de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0386" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119358} {:answer/id #uuid "7249016a-882e-5ab7-b590-5d62e12fe313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0283" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7249016a-882e-5ab7-b590-5d62e12fe313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0283" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119359} :answer-field/id #uuid "740b597a-131b-5b9f-a68c-d7b3c31da21f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119354}] :question/id #uuid "5a6c56ff-5e80-58cf-bba5-4120eb643ddb" :question/math-academy-id "q-94319" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(2, 25)$, compute $P(-0.65 < X < - 0.25)$.\n\n| $z$ | $0.03$ | $0.04$ | $0.05$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-0.5$ | $0.2981$ | $0.2946$ | $0.2912$ |\n| $-0.4$ | $0.3336$ | $0.3300$ | $0.3264$ |\n| $-0.3$ | $0.3707$ | $0.3669$ | $0.3632$ |" :question/requires-calculator true}]}
